A||||3||tiacPaul||Radiator fan switch?||||5||
Z||000000||tiacPaul||06-26-2000||01:30 AM||||I found that my duke runs bast at about 160-170 degrees.  Turning on the ac helps by keeping the fan on but I want to be able to do this without putting the engine under more load.  Anyone ever consider hooking up a dash mounted fan switch to cool off our engines before tearing off the line when they sit and build up preformace killing excess heat?||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||06-26-2000||08:52 AM||||Under the dash, above and behind the brake pedal will be a black square on the wall with many wires running through it. Near the bottom right of this square will be a green wire with a white stripe. Splice into it. Don't cut it, but take a little insulation off of it, and hook another wire to it. Run this wire to a two prong switch. One prong is hooked up to the wire you just connected to the green wire. Then hook a second wire anywhere on the frame where it will get a nice ground. Hook this wire up to the other prong on the switch. <BR>You have now just installed a manual fan switch. I have one in my Formula, the fan runs anytime I flip the switch on, until I flip it off, or turn off the ignition. Once the turn the ignition "off", the fan turns off also.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Getaway2k||06-26-2000||06:00 PM||||I too have one of these on my GT. I mounted a nice switch on the blank plate under my deck release. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||tiacPaul||06-27-2000||01:38 AM||||I KNEW I wasn't alone on this idea.  <BR>Thanks for the great directions.<BR>Now I'll be off to get my switch!<BR>||||reg||7
